PROBLEM STATEMENT: COLLEGE HOSTEL MANAGEMENT AND MAINTENANCE

BACKGROUND:
College hostels need to manage student room allocation, hostel and room information, fee payments, maintenance complaints, maintenance work, attendance, washing-machine usage and other operational records. A normalized database is required to connect these activities while keeping dependent records consistent.

CORE PROBLEMS:

Room-sharing allocation: hostel administrators need to represent one room allocation containing multiple roommates while ensuring that a student is associated with only one room allocation.
Fee tracking difficulties: fee status, due dates, amounts and payment dates need to remain linked to the relevant student and hostel.
Attendance and facility tracking: student-specific attendance and washing-machine records are dependent on the existence of the corresponding student.
Maintenance follow-up: complaints, maintenance work and the assignment relationship need to remain traceable from reporting through completion.
Different student categories: hostel administration may need to distinguish hostellers from day scholars while retaining common student information.






OBJECTIVE:
To design and implement a standalone College Hostel Management System that organizes hostel administration, shared-room allocation, fee management, complaints, maintenance work and attendance in a normalized relational database.
Maintain centralized student, hostel and room information.
Represent one ROOM_ALLOCATION per student, with each allocation capable of containing multiple roommate students through the weak ALLOCATION_MEMBER entity.
Represent STUDENT-dependent weak entities ATTENDANCE and WASHING_MACHINE.
Represent different student categories through specialization into HOSTELLER and DAY_SCHOLAR.
Track hostel fees and payment status.
Apply primary-key, foreign-key, uniqueness, domain and date-consistency constraints.

Project Design
The STUDENT table is an entity required by the hostel domain. Its presence does not make this project a subpart of a larger student database. The hostel schema is treated as a standalone domain. The added weak entities are modeled as dependent records requested for the hostel domain.

DESIRED IMPACT:
The system is intended to improve consistency of hostel records, accurately represent room occupancy by roommates, make fee and attendance information easier to retrieve, support maintenance tracking, and provide structured operational data for hostel administration.








