ENTITIES, SPECIALIZATION AND WEAK ENTITY SETS

SPECIALIZATION
STUDENT is specialized into HOSTELLER and DAY_SCHOLAR. Both subtype relations use Student_ID as their primary key and foreign key referencing STUDENTS(Student_ID).
HOSTELLER stores hostel-specific information such as Distance_From_Residence.
DAY_SCHOLAR stores Transport_Required and Arrival_Time.
The specialization is intended to distinguish students based on their residential status. The exact total/partial and disjoint/overlapping constraints should be specified according to the institution's admission rules.

WEAK ENTITY SETS
ALLOCATION_MEMBER is weak and existence-dependent on ROOM_ALLOCATION. It is identified by (Allocation_ID, Member_No).
ATTENDANCE is weak and dependent on HOSTELLER. A record is identified by (Student_ID, Attendance_Date).
Note: WASHING_MACHINE is no longer a weak entity. It is now a strong entity because washing machines are shared hostel facilities rather than machines belonging to individual students.
Similarly, ROOM_ALLOCATION is a strong entity identified independently by Allocation_ID.
KEY IDEA
A weak entity cannot be independently identified by its partial key. Its owner entity's primary key is inherited through an identifying relationship and combined with a discriminator to form its composite primary key.
In our design:
ALLOCATION_MEMBER inherits Allocation_ID from ROOM_ALLOCATION and combines it with Member_No.
ATTENDANCE inherits Student_ID from HOSTELLER and combines it with Attendance_Date.
RELATIONSHIPS, CARDINALITY AND PARTICIPATION
HOSTEL - ROOM: 1:N. One hostel can contain many rooms; each room belongs to exactly one hostel.
ROOM - ROOM_ALLOCATION: 1:N. A room can have multiple allocation records over time; each allocation refers to one room.
ROOM_ALLOCATION - ALLOCATION_MEMBER: 1:N identifying relationship. One allocation can contain multiple roommate students.
HOSTELLER - ALLOCATION_MEMBER: 1:N over time. A hosteller can have multiple allocation-member records over their stay, but only one active allocation-member record is permitted at a time.
STUDENT - HOSTELLER: 1:1 specialization relationship. A student classified as a hosteller has one corresponding HOSTELLER record.
STUDENT - DAY_SCHOLAR: 1:1 specialization relationship. A student classified as a day scholar has one corresponding DAY_SCHOLAR record.
STUDENT - USER_ACCOUNTS: 1:0..1. A student may have one login account; each student account belongs to one student.
STAFF - USER_ACCOUNTS: 1:0..1. A staff member may have one login account; each staff account belongs to one staff member.
STAFF - HOSTEL: 1:N. One staff member may act as warden for multiple hostels; each hostel can have one assigned warden.
HOSTELLER - ATTENDANCE: 1:N identifying relationship. A hosteller can have multiple attendance records over different dates.
HOSTEL - WASHING_MACHINES: 1:N. One hostel can contain multiple washing machines; each machine belongs to one hostel.
HOSTELLER - WASHING_BOOKINGS: 1:N. A hosteller can make multiple washing-machine bookings.
WASHING_MACHINES - WASHING_BOOKINGS: 1:N. One machine can receive multiple bookings over different time slots.
ALLOCATION_MEMBER - HOSTEL_FEES: 1:N. One allocation-member record can have multiple fee records.
ALLOCATION_MEMBER - COMPLAINTS: 1:N. One allocation-member record can raise multiple complaints.
COMPLAINTS - MAINTENANCE: 1:N in the original relationship design. However, the final simplified implementation allows only one maintenance record per complaint using a UNIQUE constraint on Complaint_ID.
STAFF - MAINTENANCE: 1:N. One staff member can handle multiple maintenance records; each maintenance record is assigned to one staff member.


KEYS AND REFERENTIAL INTEGRITY

The database enforces entity integrity, referential integrity, domain constraints, weak-entity identification and the room-sharing business rule using primary keys, unique constraints, foreign keys, checks and trigger/application logic where cross-row validation is required. 

KEYS AND UNIQUENESS
Strong entities use primary keys: Student_ID, Hostel_ID, Room_ID, Allocation_ID, Fee_ID, Complaint_ID, Maintenance_ID, and Assignment_ID.
HOSTELLER and DAY_SCHOLAR use Student_ID as both primary key and foreign key.
ATTENDANCE uses (Student_ID, Attendance_Date) as primary key.
WASHING_MACHINE uses (Student_ID, Machine_No, Usage_Date).
STUDENT.Email is UNIQUE.
STUDENT.Age is a derived attribute calculated from DOB and is not stored. HOSTELLER.Distance_From_Residence exists only for hostellers.


REFERENTIAL INTEGRITY
ROOM.Hostel_ID references HOSTEL(Hostel_ID).
ROOM_ALLOCATION.Room_ID references ROOM(Room_ID).
All weak entities reference their owner entity through NOT NULL foreign keys.
HOSTEL_FEE references STUDENT and HOSTEL.
COMPLAINT references STUDENT and ROOM.
MAINTENANCE references COMPLAINT.
HOSTEL_FEES references ALLOCATION_MEMBER.
COMPLAINTS references ALLOCATION_MEMBER.
WASHING_BOOKINGS references HOSTELLER and WASHING_MACHINE.










